[13:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, there are two main lessons that I got from this experience that I want to share with you today regarding mindset. The first is that you need to be focused on the right things. The main reason I burnt out is because I was focused on money. Had I instead been focusing on'

[14:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'improving my craft or flushing out my worlds, perhaps I would have felt differently about the whole ordeal because those factors were more in my control. The fact that I was focused on money which is outside my control, at least outside my direct control, that'

[14:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'meant my feelings of accomplishment were contingent on an external factor. So, as we go into this course, I recommend not focusing so much on the money. Money is definitely important, but I would go into this assuming that you\'re not going'

[14:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to make money for a while until you\'ve truly mastered the craft and until you have a lot of high rated books out. And it can take some time to get there and you will need to be willing to do this. And I say this not just as an AI writer,'

[14:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but as a writer in general, it\'s going to take time. The chances of a one-hit wonder are almost zero. Now, the people who succeed at this have been doing it for a long time. That means you need to do this as well. Even if you\'re using'

[15:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'AI, there is a lot you need to learn about craft and story and structure and character arts, you name it. So, if you focus instead on becoming a master storyteller, then every time you craft a story, you are going to be succeeding in'

[15:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that goal. Whereas if you focus on the the there is a good chance that you won\'t be succeeding on that goal for potentially years to come. And there\'s no faster path to burnout than not succeeding at what you perceive to be success. All you have to do is redefine'

[15:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your definition of success and you can avoid burnout. The second lesson I want to share that I got from my burnout experience is this. Focus more on those things that light you up. In any small business, you are going to have to do'

[15:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'some things that you find unpleasant and that\'s just part of it. But the more of these things that you can eliminate or outsource to other people the better. And AI has made this so much easier since its explosion onto the landscape. I know some writing purists are going to'

[15:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get really mad to hear me say this, but I don\'t and never really did like the actual writing process. [music] And I mean the butt-in-chair typing on the keyboard part of the creative process. And writing, especially creative'

[16:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'writing, is a lot more than just one step. It includes, but is not limited to, brainstorming, character development, plotting, outlining, research, and staring at a blank window, working with a developmental editor, working with a line editor, working with'

[16:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a proofreader, getting your book cover designed, understanding genre and markets, understanding tropes and what readers actually want, learning to market your books, which yes, is a creative process in its own way. And there could be many more specific to the'

[16:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'type of books you want to write. For instance, because a lot of my books are set in Arthurian England, I\'ve had a blast just researching that era and all of the different myths and legends associated with it. My point is, writing is a multi-step process. And if we want'

[16:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to consider this like a business, we absolutely must focus on those things that interest us the most or that we are the most skilled at so that we can spend as much time doing those things as possible. Everything else, we should'

[16:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'find ways to lessen the amount of work we have to do in whatever way we can, whether that\'s eliminating it entirely, sometimes there are things we just don\'t need that maybe we thought we did, or outsourcing it to another person, or'

[17:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'outsourcing it to AI. For this course, I will be showing you how to use AI at practically every step of the process, but you definitely do not have to use it for every step. Instead, use it for that area that you dislike the most, the part'

[17:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that if you had to do it over and over and over again might burn you out over time. Now, lastly, before we get into our next module, I want to mention two other mindset shifts that are going to be the difference between success and failure in a storytelling driven'

[17:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'business. The first is that you do need to treat this like a business. Now, I\'m absolutely fine if you just want to write a book for your kids or your grandkids or even for yourself, but I would not expect that to sell. And it\'s'

[17:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'also totally fine if you want to treat this as a hobby. More power to you. But, if we want to be able to continue to create art of any kind in a way that allows us to work on it full-time, we must get paid eventually. I know I said earlier that we don\'t want to focus on'

[18:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the money, and we don\'t. We want to focus on growth because growth will lead to money. But, we can\'t kid ourselves and say that we can do this full-time without having some kind of income. Keep in mind that success isn\'t always the road we planned from the start, but at'

[18:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'some point we must get an income from our efforts, otherwise we\'ll be forced to continue with these hobbies as just that, a hobby. You must treat this like a business, and a big part of that is learning about investments. And I\'m not talking about investing in the stock'

[18:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'market or anything like that. I\'m talking about investing in yourself and in the business. [music] A lot of people look at what it costs to create a business. And while it is important to be frugal, especially early on when you\'re starting a business, [music] and'

[18:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to do much of the work yourself, doing things that free up your time to work on that which you\'re best at, or spending money on education to give you the best possible start to your journey, all of that could be considered an investment. For instance, I recently invested $8,000'

[18:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of my own money into an organization that would help me identify good YouTube video topics to target. And that may sound like a lot for something so simple. But, already I\'ve seen huge returns from that investment, and you'

[19:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have two things to invest, money and time. Which one you invest will depend on which one you have more of. But, the idea of investing is this: you put in the time or the money and hopefully by'

[19:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'doing those things you get returns that are greater than what you put into it. But, creating a business will require investment and that is very important. Now, the second mindset shift that I want to hammer home for you is the'

[19:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'difference between an abundance and a scarcity mindset. A scarcity mindset is a habitual focus on what you don\'t have, combined with the belief that resources and opportunities or success are limited so that other people\'s wins feels like'

[19:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your loss. On the other hand, an abundance mindset is a belief that there are enough resources and opportunities for everyone and that you can create more value through learning and collaboration and experimentation. This is one of the core issues that plague'

[19:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'most authors. Common scarcity thoughts for aspiring authors include the market is saturated so no one will ever find my book. Or, if that author in my niche succeeds, they\'re taking my readers. Or, I can\'t invest in covers, editing, or'

[20:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ads until I\'m already making money. I only get one shot with this book. If it doesn\'t blow up, I\'ve failed. That was my biggest mindset issue early on. Now, psychologically a scarcity mindset narrows your attention, pushes people into high-stakes thinking, and triggers'

[20:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'anxiety and avoidance behaviors. And it\'s just not pleasant. Now, most successful entrepreneurs that I know have an abundance mindset. Now, this idea is that there\'s more than enough and that we actually create value instead of using it up. An abundance'

[20:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mindset doesn\'t pretend that resources are infinite, but it does focus on opportunities and levers that you control. For authors, that sounds like this: There are always more readers entering my genre and I can reach them over time with multiple books. Other'

[20:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'authors\' success proves that there\'s demand, so we can cross-pollinate audiences. Every dollar I invest, when I can afford it, is a test of return on investment, not a permanent loss. Or, this book is one step in a long-term'

[20:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'catalog. Even a failed launch gives data that I can use later. Abundance thinking invites experimentation as well as long-term strategy and collaboration instead of perfectionism or jealousy or isolation. It also helps you become an'

[21:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'opportunity seeker, making you far more likely to spot opportunities when those with a scarcity mindset would simply brush them off. This is why those who have an abundance and a growth mindset actually are proven in studies to be luckier compared to those without. Now,'

