[81:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that we\'ve pretty much covered all of the tools that we\'re going to be using inside of this full course, let\'s move on to module three, where we\'re going to talk about what I think is the most overlooked part of this process. [music] Pretty much all of the other AI writing'

[81:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'software tools out there ignore this step, and I think it\'s one of those steps that has really helped my work excel. So let\'s get into that now. Welcome to module three, where we\'re going to talk about one of the aspects'

[81:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of AI writing that, as far as I know, originated with me on this channel. Most of the AI writing tools out there give you the tools to create your characters and plot and world building, but all of them miss one important step has to come'

[81:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'first. Because you see, if AI isn\'t guided well, when you go to ask it for all of those assets, it will give you very generic suggestions. And this is where actual AI slop comes from, right?'

[81:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'People who just went to the defaults that AI gave them. But something I realized is that if you give AI a solid framework to build on, ideally one that is specifically designed for whatever genre you\'re writing in, cuz not all'

[82:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'genres are written the same. You can\'t use the Hero\'s Journey framework for a romance book, for example. And even a more romance specific framework like Romancing the Beat might not work for certain subgenres or of romance. Or the very least, those subgenres would have'

[82:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'additional requirements. So, an [music] important first step to the AI writing process is not to plot your outline or start fleshing out your characters. It\'s actually to just establish the templates that you were going to follow. Because'

[82:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'if you don\'t do that, you\'ll end up with generic AI slop. Now, I first figured this out a long time ago, and so I spent quite a bit of time handcrafting the perfect 40-chapter template for a'

[82:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'general book. And that ended up becoming this book right here called the Plot Module. This book came about because I found an as I was reading a lot of books about story structure, a lot of them had the same different beats. And some of'

[83:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'them even told you the approximate number of scenes that you should be going for. Save the Cat, for instance, gave me 15 beats, but said that you should have roughly 40 scenes for a book. And I actually found that this'

[83:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'number of 40 scenes or 40 chapters came up quite a bit, not just in screenwriting, but in books about structure for novels as well. And so I thought, well, okay, if there\'s 15 beats in the case of Save the Cat, and there\'s'

[83:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '40 scenes, what are happening in the other 25 scenes, right? So, I went to to study that whole thing and really went chapter by chapter trying to figure out what should if you had 40 scenes, like'

[83:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'let\'s just cap it at 40, what would you put in each one? And the result of that became this book. And it\'s still today my best-selling book of all of them, fiction and nonfiction. Does very well for me. And that was the initial attempt'

[83:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at creating a template that could be really useful and specific for AI writing. I mean, it\'s useful for human writing as well, cuz it\'s nice to have a'

[84:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'framework to follow. The problem, the only problem with this is that it it was a bit even this is a little too generic and doesn\'t fit every genre. But at the very least, this will probably teach you good story structure better than any'

[84:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'other book, because it actually walks you through chapter by a like that. But because it\'s not that genre-specific, I came up with a different way that Well, actually, me and my community came up with a different way of getting a little'

[84:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bit more nuance in your templates. And that leads me to talk about the story hacking process. So, to briefly go over this, this is an automation I made in n8n that story hacks a book. I\'m not'

[84:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to go into too much depth here, but I\'m just trying to give you an idea. But, basically, what it does is these steps, it grabs a file. So, for instance, you can go online and find screenplays from, you know, thousands of'

[84:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'films. You grab one of those, and then you kind of highlight where the scene breaks are. You do That is a preparatory step you have to do. You highlight the scene breaks and make sure that your heading for each scene is formatted in a'

[85:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'specific way. And then you pull that document in here in with these two steps. And then assuming you formatted the document correctly, it will then essentially chop it up into those individual scenes. And then for each'

[85:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scene, it runs a summary prompt on it. Kind of see what\'s happening in the scene. Now, this is more than just a summary. If you check it out, there\'s a lot here. So, we\'re determining, let\'s say, the So, we have the summary, then'

[85:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
