[97:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are really just a starting point. The best way to become familiar with a genre is to become well-read in that genre. So, I recommend simply doing a lot of perusing, and that could be on Amazon, at bookstores, and in libraries. Read a'

[97:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lot of books and figure out what makes a difference between how those books are positioned online, how they\'re marketed, what kind of book cover elements do they have in common, et cetera. I found that most authors, and my earlier self included, tend to not niche down enough.'

[97:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You might know that you want to write a clean romance, for example, but don\'t realize that there are actually dozens of subgenres within that category. I found that simply spending a few hours browsing on Amazon can actually give you a good idea of what people are buying,'

[98:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and you could quickly figure out that maybe instead of just clean romance, a better niche might be clean Christian Amish small-town romance with an enemies-to-lovers trope. The nice thing about doing all that genre research is that it does half of the brainstorming'

[98:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'process for you. You no longer have to come up with all of the elements because the reader expectations for that genre and those tropes already mandate a few things. It\'s now just your responsibility to fill in the rest of the gaps so that your book becomes a'

[98:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fresh take on that genre. Remember, we\'re not trying to be original here. No purely original book has ever sold widely at all, ever. What we want instead is fresh, a spin on something that readers are already familiar with'

[98:38] [scene:talk]
