[159:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'automation, it makes sense and this will actually improve the quality of your chapter significantly. So, let\'s just briefly run through what this automation does. First, I bring in a pro style sheet and this is something that you'

[159:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'could add here into your cloud project as well. I haven\'t talked about creating style sheets, but I have whole videos devoted to creating a style sheet. You could bring in simply examples of your writing and do it that way, but we bring that in, we bring in the character'

[159:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sheet, the world building sheet and the outline. We create a new blank document where this book is going to go and then this step sorts through all of the chapters in the outline, creates a list of each one and then it starts going'

[159:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'through a loop to go through all of those chapters and write them one at a time. Before it does that, it grabs the previous 2,000 words. Now, of course, if you\'re writing chapter one, you don\'t have any previous words, so this is'

[159:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'moot, but if you\'re writing chapter two, this is where it\'s getting the previous chapter text from. So, it will always have the previous 2,000 words as an example of what you\'re writing and what you want it to look like. And then, we'

[160:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'zoom out just a little bit, all of these steps that you see right here are all devoted to creating one thing, which is a scene brief. And I used to do this in one step, but what I wanted to do is to think more carefully about each step.'

[160:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, I separated it all into multiple steps so that I could actually more accurately think through everything it needs and create the scene brief so that when it goes to write the first draft,'

[160:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that scene brief is able to guide it in a much more efficient way. So, we start with these three steps here, which are the plot selector, the character selector and the world building selector. And all these do is they look through the like, okay, we\'re going to'

[160:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'write chapter one here. Let\'s look through all of the elements that are going to be needed for writing chapter one. And so, it looks through, it grabs the plot of that chapter from the outline. It grabs the characters, only'

[160:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the characters that are showing up in that scene. It grabs the world building elements that are relevant to that scene, so it\'s only grabbing those. And I\'m using really cheap models here so that this is not using a lot of context. And even though this adds extra steps,'

[161:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this is actually going to save us tokens. And then I ask it to estimate the word count here in a two-step process. Basically, all that happens here is that estimates the word count and then multiplies it by 1.5 because usually it needs to be a little bit'

[161:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'higher than it thinks it does. And then we run into these three steps, which is taking what we selected from the plot, the characters, and the world building, and it fleshes them out a little bit more and makes it appropriate for the'

[161:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scene. So, this plot scene brief will flesh out everything. It\'ll give me a bunch of beats of the scene and blocking. Blocking is a really underrated thing when it comes to writing with AI. The concept of blocking is that figuring out where in the the'

[161:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'physical space are all your characters and objects and things and kind of determining that because AI doesn\'t often do this naturally. It\'ll just sort of have what we call it the floating head phenomenon where these characters'

[162:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are just sort of existing in the scene, but we\'re not really given a whole lot of context of where they are in the scene, which can help ground it a little bit more. So, it does that and establishes blocking. It also establishes the character scene brief.'

[162:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, what this does is it takes all of the characters that were selected that are going to be relevant for this scene and it adjusts them as needed. I\'ve already mentioned the sliders earlier in this course. The sliders like are they'

[162:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'calm or are they stressed? This will adjust those sliders depending on the scene and what\'s going on in the scene. So, if it\'s a really stressful scene, it might adjust the sliders to be more stressful. If it\'s more of a relaxing'

[162:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scene, then it might make them a little bit more calm. And so on for all of the the sliders. It\'ll also adjust things like what are they wearing and other things that might be relevant to that scene. Like let\'s say your character'

[162:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'loses an arm halfway through the book. If that\'s the case, then we don\'t want them to be described as having both arms for all of the chapters that exist after that. So, this kind of goes through and finds all of those things and adjust'

[163:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'them to make sure that everything works for those characters. And then this final step in this process, it does the exact same thing but for the world building instead of the characters. And then we run our first chronology check. What this does is it goes through and'

[163:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'establishes that, "Hey, looks at the scene brief. It looks at everything that\'s been generated here." And it compares that to the overall outline and says, "Are there any issues with chronology here? Are we like introducing'

[163:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'clues that shouldn\'t be revealed yet?" That sort of thing. And then it does a rewrite of the scene brief from all of that info that it gives us. And then we finally, after all of that, move on to'

[163:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'writing the first draft. And ironically, with all of those steps that we had to create the the scene brief, we really only need this one to write the first draft. It\'ll take that scene brief and write the whole thing. And then I have'

[163:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it take that and look at the last 20,000 words. And from there it\'ll do a second chronology check, where basically it\'s looking at everything that was written previously, or at least the last 20,000 words, and make sure there is no major'

[164:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'chronology issues from those previous chapters to this one. It\'ll also do a style check, so this will compare your style guide against the what was written in the first draft, which by the way we bring in that style guide at to give it'

[164:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'advice on how to write the first draft. But this just does a double check of that style to make sure that everything\'s good there. it does, from that all of the data that it collects from that, it does a uh another rewrite and adds that chapter to your document.'

[164:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And from that, that\'s the end of this automation. So, it\'s a very involved automation, and it does this for every chapter, and it can sometimes take hours to go through the whole thing. And I wouldn\'t actually go through the whole thing. I would do one or two or three'

[164:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'chapters at a time at most, and then go back to your outline, make sure that the next couple of chapters are are good and have what you want, and then you can let it go for the next few chapters and just continue that cycle until you\'ve'

[164:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'finished the whole book. So, what does this look like inside of Story Hacker OS? If we move on to that. Now, unlike the other steps, I do not have a prose writing persona in my list of personas because of the complexity of this whole'

