[167:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get fun and for many people it\'s the far more creative part of the writing process. I personally don\'t enjoy it as much as the outlining stage but I know a lot of writers will spend most [music] of their time in the editing stage to'

[167:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really perfect that book. Even authors who don\'t use AI, in fact, especially authors that don\'t use AI uh spend a lot of time in the editing. Now traditionally there have been approximately three types of editing.'

[167:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Some people say four but they split hairs and I like to keep things simple. The first is developmental editing. This is the kind of editing where you will, especially if you have a developmental editor, they\'ll actually go through your'

[168:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'entire book and look at it from a structural standpoint. They\'ll look at character arcs. They\'ll look at making sure everything is like logically consistent within the book. Make sure that you don\'t need any additional scenes here and there or maybe you need'

[168:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to cut a few scenes here or there because they don\'t really serve the overall plot. Regardless, that\'s what a developmental editor does. Quick tip, if you hire a developmental editor, I recommend doing so before you\'ve written the book while you\'re in the outlining'

[168:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stage because first of all, it will cost a whole lot less for them to go through your outline and give you advice there than it will to have them go through your entire book. But, I digress. The next type is called line editing, and'

[168:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this is editing that takes place on a more sentence-by-sentence, paragraph-by-paragraph basis. [music] Where we\'re actually looking at the structure of the sentence and the paragraph, and actually going through and trying to make improvements there.'

[168:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Funny enough, this is very similar to what we will be doing with the AI-generated text. The third type of editing is proofreading. A lot of people will also throw in copyediting here. I find that there\'s not a good definition'

[169:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of like where line editing ends and where copywriting or proofreading begin. So, for me, it\'s easier to differentiate between line editing and proofreading. But, proofreading is essentially just going through and making those more'

[169:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'technical, grammatical, or spelling changes to make sure that there\'s nothing actually like grammatically wrong with your book. AI is really good at a lot of things, and it can actually save you a lot of time in the edit. For'

[169:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'instance, proofreading is something AI is extremely good at. And that alone could save you hundreds of dollars on a proofreader. And it already does a decent job at line editing compared to the average human. Or at least, what it writes is not too bad compared to the'

[169:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'average human. Though, it still has a ways to go before it\'s the same as a skilled human, especially a skilled human that has had a line edit of their work. But, while AI-written books are already a step above what you might get'

[170:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'if you wrote the book yourself, especially if you\'re a beginner, writing with AI does introduce some new problems that come with ones that it solves. And I\'m talking, of course, of those AI-isms, those repetitive words or phrases that AI tends to introduce while'

[170:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s writing, because of the way it\'s trained. Now, don\'t get me wrong, even though this is a new problem that didn\'t exist before, I still think AI saves you a lot of time overall. In fact, I don\'t think it, I know it. And eventually,'

[170:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'these models will improve so that these issues are no longer as big of a problem. But, for now, we do have to deal with these AI-isms. So, here\'s is of the top 10 AI-isms so you can look out for them. The first is the'

[170:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'contrastive hook. This is where you say, "It\'s not X, it\'s Y." It\'s a neat little rhythm where the text negates one or two things and then reframes them creating a slick but overproduced slogan-like intro. The second is a rhetorical'

[170:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'question with an instant answer. The writing poses a question as a fake hook like, "What does this really mean?" and then immediately answers it instead of letting the question genuinely drive exploration. The third is the triple'

[171:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'emphasis lists, things like faster, smarter, better. Ideas are constantly packaged into a three-part list for cadence, even when the content doesn\'t naturally fall into threes. The fourth is overused AI words. You\'ve probably'

[171:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'heard of delve or leverage or holistic or testament. You\'ll hear a lot of these things. We\'ve got big lists of them that you can find all over the internet. The prose leans heavily on a small set of smart-sounding abstractions that show up'

[171:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'far more often in machine-generated text than in normal human writing. Number five is inflated stakes for mundane topics. So, everyday subjects get framed as sweeping paradigm shifts making small points sound like civilizational turning'

[171:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'points. Going along that is number six with grand abstract metaphors. So, things like the tapestry of or the landscape of or the a journey through. Abstract discussions are draped in clichéd metaphorical language that'

[171:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sounds lyrical but rarely adds concrete meaning. The seventh is generic importance adjectives. Things like significant, impactful, or key. These claims signal importance with vague, overused adjectives instead of precise'

[172:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] descriptors or measurable details. Number eight is bland, high-level generalizations. So, this could be like the writing hovers at the summary level and avoids specific numbers, names, locations, or sensory'

[172:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'detail. So, the prose feels kind of floaty and ungrounded. You\'ll also see this with this is why I prompt so heavily to try and get it into a show-don\'t-tell state. Otherwise, it can feel very lofty and very much like we\'re'

[172:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just telling instead of showing. Number nine, you\'ll also see repetitive sentence templates and rephrasing. Though, the same few sentence shapes and phrases can reoccur restating the same idea in slightly different words instead of advancing the thought itself. This is'

[172:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how you can get really wordy AI output. Number 10 is overly neat and bow-tied conclusions. This is an ending recap everything and lands on a polished inspirational line that reads more like'

[172:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a generic slogan than a natural closing thought. And you\'ll see this at the end of chapters as well, but this is a particularly bad problem in things like academic writing. So, with that out of the way, let\'s now take a look at some AI writing so I can give you an idea of'

[173:09] [scene:talk]
